{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789458216,"nonce":"RssSPYVvsFS8K/a6Ok1N/6Ez+5U=","title":"Python: tuple() of a typed list kept the typed layout under the tuple type","message":"`tuple(xs)` for `xs: list[int]` or `list[str]` copied the typed list and retyped the copy as a tuple, whose elements are dynamic values. Reading `t[0]` or printing it then read an integer or a string pointer as a box: `print(tuple([3, 1, 4]))` and `print(tuple(ys))` with `ys = [str(x) for x in xs]` died with SIGSEGV; `len(t)` was right. Only `tuple()` of an already dynamic list or a literal worked.\n\nFix: a typed source is boxed into a fresh `list[object]` before the copy, the way a list of int flows into any other dynamic slot.","files":null}]}